WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.393
Let me tell you right off that
I did not invent this field.

00:00:10.393 --> 00:00:12.858
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.858 --> 00:00:16.470
We've detected
gravitational waves

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:19.930
from this crashing of two
black holes into each other.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:22.000
This is the beginning
of a whole new way

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.240
of studying the universe.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:27.760
It's like Galileo using the
telescope for the first time.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.300
What is a gravitational wave?

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.650
It's an excitation of
space and time together.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:34.630
Nobody really believed
that you could ever

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:38.668
detect them because the size
of the effect is so small.

00:00:38.668 --> 00:00:42.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:45.020
The idea was extremely simple.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.580
What the gravitational wave
does is stretches space this way

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:50.900
and compresses space that way.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.260
So you exploit that property.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:56.360
Put one object here and
another object over there,

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.440
and let the gravitational
wave go through that system.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:01.900
And it'll change the
space between these

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:05.250
by contracting that one
and extending that one.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.910
And I came to the
conclusion that,

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:08.560
if you made this long enough--

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:11.050
kilometer scale--
you could probably

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:14.059
get these extremely
precise measurements.

00:01:14.059 --> 00:01:18.051
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.750
I said, "Holy mackerel!"

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:36.940
We have shown that Einstein--

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:40.830
God knows how he got
it-- was right in 1915.

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.410
To me, that's a miracle
that that happened.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.180
The man is thinking--

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:46.436
and I don't mean the theory--

00:01:46.436 --> 00:01:47.936
but the elegance
in the experiment--

00:01:47.936 --> 00:01:51.030
I mean, that is a human
endeavor that, I think,

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.580
everybody in the world
should be proud of.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.940
Congratulations, we
are thrilled for you.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:59.615
And we're thrilled for all
those who collaborated with you.

00:01:59.615 --> 00:02:00.240
And guess what?

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.370
You know what I think?

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:03.680
You are the best of MIT, Rai.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.680
Thank you and congratulations.

